    "q-127305": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127305",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-4xy,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129319/assets/9e0bf7b88eadd199edc1997410b7ddbaa74b204e7d0f8afef7c9b17c28d8f8dc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{x}^{2}-4yx]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[({1}^{2}-4y(1))-((-1)^{2}-4y(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}-8y\\,dy \\\\  & =-4{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-4(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
