    "q-127370": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127370",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨-x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y+1,$ $\\,x={e}^{y},$ and the line $y=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129319/assets/728036ffc90e2476017b3102b6279f9c5900f0e7fc58536e76c9aa038eb04f02.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y+1\\le x\\le {e}^{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{-y+1}^{{e}^{y}}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}+y-1)\\,dy \\\\  & =-{[{e}^{y}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-[(e+\\frac{1}{2}-1)-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
