    "q-127328": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127328",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+y,\\,xy+1⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y,$ $\\,x=\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129319/assets/2ecabe22395006f0c7ef0a3f914087fa324f4d59c7d7886c18907f824744643e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}3x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
